1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,664
Japan's economy is sliding to sixth in the world.

2
00:00:02,664 --> 00:00:04,930
So why did Warren Buffett buy Japanese stocks?

3
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:08,001
The IMF's new outlook this month has Japan as

4
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,070
the world's fourth-largest economy today.

5
00:00:10,870 --> 00:00:13,373
Next year India and the U.K. pass it,

6
00:00:13,373 --> 00:00:15,064
and Japan drops to sixth,

7
00:00:15,064 --> 00:00:16,890
its lowest rank since 1968.

8
00:00:17,690 --> 00:00:20,777
Yet the five Japanese stocks Berkshire Hathaway

9
00:00:20,777 --> 00:00:23,865
began buying in 2020 were worth 2.3 times their

10
00:00:23,865 --> 00:00:25,770
cost by the end of last year.

11
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:28,949
Over six years it put in 15.4 billion dollars.

12
00:00:28,949 --> 00:00:32,000
By the end of last year the stakes were worth 35.4 billion.

13
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,530
And that's before any dividends.

14
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:37,810
Stay to the end for three things.

15
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,310
One: the five companies revealed on Buffett's 90th birthday.

16
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:43,910
Two: why he borrowed money in Japan.

17
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:47,555
Three: how companies grow while their country shrinks,

18
00:00:47,555 --> 00:00:49,660
and how to check your own stocks.

19
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:51,861
The country is shrinking,

20
00:00:51,861 --> 00:00:54,269
yet its companies' stock more than doubled.

21
00:00:54,269 --> 00:00:55,950
Is one of these numbers wrong?

22
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:58,270
No. Both are right.

23
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:00,842
The timing is even stranger.

24
00:01:00,842 --> 00:01:02,793
It began in the summer of 2020,

25
00:01:02,793 --> 00:01:05,940
when few were paying attention to Japanese stocks.

26
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:10,310
A 90-year-old investor was quietly buying five Japanese companies.

27
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:14,250
It starts on Sunday morning, August 30, 2020.

28
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,099
It was Buffett's 90th birthday.

29
00:01:17,099 --> 00:01:19,690
Berkshire put out a short announcement.

30
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:22,978
It had bought slightly more than 5% of each of

31
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:25,461
five Japanese trading houses: Itochu,

32
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:28,280
Marubeni, Mitsubishi, Mitsui and Sumitomo.

33
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:30,915
Over twelve months on the Tokyo exchange,

34
00:01:30,915 --> 00:01:33,410
quietly, worth about 6.25 billion dollars.

35
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:36,625
Most people get gifts on their 90th birthday.

36
00:01:36,625 --> 00:01:38,636
Buffett went shopping himself,

37
00:01:38,636 --> 00:01:41,450
and his cart held five Japanese companies.

38
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:43,619
They were cheap.

39
00:01:43,619 --> 00:01:47,640
Four of the five traded below their net assets.

40
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:51,600
Like a shop selling 10,000 won of assets for 6,000 to 8,000 won.

41
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,320
Only Itochu traded a bit higher,

42
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:54,460
at 1.3 times net assets.

43
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:59,430
But what exactly does a Japanese trading house do?

44
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:01,667
They started as traders,

45
00:02:01,667 --> 00:02:04,840
buying what Japan needed and selling what Japan made.

46
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:05,584
Oil, wheat,

47
00:02:05,584 --> 00:02:08,750
iron ore: much of it came into Japan through the trading houses.

48
00:02:09,540 --> 00:02:11,041
Today they don't just trade.

49
00:02:11,041 --> 00:02:13,186
They own pieces of Chilean copper mines,

50
00:02:13,186 --> 00:02:13,990
Australian coal

51
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:16,285
and liquefied natural gas projects,

52
00:02:16,285 --> 00:02:18,200
and take a share of the profits.

53
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,493
Japan's convenience stores, too.

54
00:02:21,493 --> 00:02:24,920
Mitsubishi and KDDI each own half of Lawson.

55
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:28,127
Itochu's group owns nearly all of FamilyMart.

56
00:02:28,127 --> 00:02:29,792
Buy a rice ball in Tokyo,

57
00:02:29,792 --> 00:02:31,990
and a trading house books a line.

58
00:02:32,790 --> 00:02:34,556
Buffett wrote that these companies very

59
00:02:34,556 --> 00:02:36,412
successfully operate in a manner somewhat

60
00:02:36,412 --> 00:02:37,680
similar to Berkshire itself.

61
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:38,788
Highly diversified, he noted,

62
00:02:38,788 --> 00:02:40,009
with shareholder-friendly policies much

63
00:02:40,009 --> 00:02:41,230
superior to those customary in the U.S.

64
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:45,140
So, part one: five little Berkshires,

65
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:48,826
owning mines, energy and convenience stores,

66
00:02:48,826 --> 00:02:52,010
and returning profits to shareholders.

67
00:02:52,210 --> 00:02:55,699
But around then the yen weakened sharply again,

68
00:02:55,699 --> 00:02:59,040
and Japan shrank even faster in dollar terms.

69
00:02:59,830 --> 00:03:01,780
Picture an office worker in Tokyo.

70
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:04,680
Say the salary is 300,000 yen a month.

71
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:08,245
In 2012 a dollar bought about 80 yen,

72
00:03:08,245 --> 00:03:10,610
so that was 3,750 dollars.

73
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:12,675
Now a dollar buys around 157 yen,

74
00:03:12,675 --> 00:03:14,880
so the same pay is about 1,900 dollars.

75
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,289
Same salary, half the dollars.

76
00:03:17,289 --> 00:03:20,970
Like the same height on a ruler with new markings.

77
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:24,681
The whole country, too. Measured in dollars,

78
00:03:24,681 --> 00:03:27,734
Japan's economy went from 6.3 trillion in 2012

79
00:03:27,734 --> 00:03:29,460
to 4.3 trillion this year.

80
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:31,520
Nearly a third smaller.

81
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:35,000
Japan became the world's number two economy in 1968.

82
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:37,831
It lost that spot to China in 2010,

83
00:03:37,831 --> 00:03:39,560
and to Germany in 2023.

84
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:42,140
Next year it's projected to be sixth.

85
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:46,758
That doesn't simply mean people got that much poorer.

86
00:03:46,758 --> 00:03:50,000
GDP rankings convert everything into dollars,

87
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,450
so a weak yen drags the rank down.

88
00:03:52,650 --> 00:03:55,191
A shrinking population and slow growth play a part,

89
00:03:55,191 --> 00:03:55,390
too.

90
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,958
So why did the yen weaken so much?

91
00:03:58,958 --> 00:04:02,691
Largely because Japan kept rates near zero for

92
00:04:02,691 --> 00:04:04,800
years to escape deflation.

93
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,627
Money flowed to America,

94
00:04:06,627 --> 00:04:08,524
where rates were far higher,

95
00:04:08,524 --> 00:04:11,100
so people sold yen and bought dollars.

96
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:12,889
But for some companies,

97
00:04:12,889 --> 00:04:15,790
that shrinking ruler worked the other way.

98
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:19,420
Remember how the trading houses make money?

99
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:23,318
Much of their money is earned abroad in dollars: Australian coal,

100
00:04:23,318 --> 00:04:24,700
Chilean copper, American LNG.

101
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:27,216
Say they earn 100 million dollars abroad.

102
00:04:27,216 --> 00:04:28,403
At 80 yen per dollar,

103
00:04:28,403 --> 00:04:30,550
the books in Japan show 8 billion yen.

104
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:34,591
At 157 yen, it's 15.7 billion. Same business,

105
00:04:34,591 --> 00:04:37,330
nearly double the profit in yen.

106
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:40,030
Helped partly by the weak yen,

107
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:42,927
Japanese companies' profits from April to June

108
00:04:42,927 --> 00:04:44,880
rose 24.6% from a year earlier.

109
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:47,028
A record quarterly amount,

110
00:04:47,028 --> 00:04:50,100
according to Japan's Ministry of Finance.

111
00:04:50,890 --> 00:04:52,485
The country is graded in dollars,

112
00:04:52,485 --> 00:04:54,031
the companies keep books in yen,

113
00:04:54,031 --> 00:04:56,350
so the two numbers moved in opposite directions.

114
00:04:56,550 --> 00:05:02,447
And Buffett made one more move: he borrowed the money in Japan,

115
00:05:02,447 --> 00:05:04,880
in that same low-rate yen.

116
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:09,230
Part two: why borrow in Japan?

117
00:05:10,030 --> 00:05:14,058
Berkshire didn't send dollars from America.

118
00:05:14,058 --> 00:05:18,180
It sold yen bonds in Japan and borrowed yen.

119
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:23,040
Greg Abel says it borrowed roughly as much yen as it invested.

120
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:24,509
If the yen falls,

121
00:05:24,509 --> 00:05:27,102
the stocks are worth fewer dollars,

122
00:05:27,102 --> 00:05:28,510
but so is the debt.

123
00:05:28,710 --> 00:05:32,442
Like buying a house abroad with a loan from a local bank.

124
00:05:32,442 --> 00:05:34,930
Most of the currency risk cancels out.

125
00:05:35,730 --> 00:05:38,840
In his 2024 letter Buffett expected about 812

126
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:41,398
million dollars of dividends in 2025,

127
00:05:41,398 --> 00:05:45,130
against about 135 million of interest on the yen debt.

128
00:05:45,330 --> 00:05:48,036
Dividends about six times the interest.

129
00:05:48,036 --> 00:05:52,060
Abel's letter puts the average yen borrowing cost at 1.2%.

130
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:56,096
The machine is still running: this April,

131
00:05:56,096 --> 00:05:58,226
after Buffett stepped down,

132
00:05:58,226 --> 00:06:02,330
Berkshire issued another 272.3 billion yen of bonds.

133
00:06:03,130 --> 00:06:07,506
That's part two: borrow in yen to cut currency risk,

134
00:06:07,506 --> 00:06:10,536
pay 135 million dollars in interest,

135
00:06:10,536 --> 00:06:13,650
and collect 812 million in dividends.

136
00:06:13,850 --> 00:06:15,048
So six years on,

137
00:06:15,048 --> 00:06:17,520
what does Berkshire's ledger say?

138
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:20,120
Let's line up the numbers.

139
00:06:20,920 --> 00:06:23,703
In August 2020, slightly over 5% each,

140
00:06:23,703 --> 00:06:25,680
about 6.25 billion dollars.

141
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:29,136
By the end of last year, worth 35.4 billion,

142
00:06:29,136 --> 00:06:32,170
with 15.4 billion invested along the way.

143
00:06:32,370 --> 00:06:35,670
Ownership is now around 10% in all five.

144
00:06:36,470 --> 00:06:36,978
Greg Abel,

145
00:06:36,978 --> 00:06:39,520
who took over as CEO from Buffett in January 2026,

146
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:41,960
wrote about Japan at length in his first letter.

147
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:44,805
He called the Japan stakes comparable to Berkshire's major U.S.

148
00:06:44,805 --> 00:06:45,770
holdings in importance.

149
00:06:45,970 --> 00:06:50,795
Buffett had written that they'd be held for many decades.

150
00:06:50,795 --> 00:06:53,250
New boss, same favorite shop.

151
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:55,085
Last year alone,

152
00:06:55,085 --> 00:06:57,240
862 million dollars in dividends.

153
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:01,180
About 5.6% of the money invested, in one year.

154
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:03,887
It isn't always smooth.

155
00:07:03,887 --> 00:07:07,202
Trading-house profits swing with copper,

156
00:07:07,202 --> 00:07:08,860
coal and LNG prices.

157
00:07:09,060 --> 00:07:11,079
If the yen suddenly strengthens,

158
00:07:11,079 --> 00:07:13,350
profits earned abroad shrink in yen.

159
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:17,209
And the 2.3 times includes some luck of timing.

160
00:07:17,209 --> 00:07:20,470
Summer 2020 was an unusually cheap moment.

161
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:24,510
So how does the same company look on today's numbers?

162
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:27,606
Let's put the two dates side by side with ValueNote,

163
00:07:27,606 --> 00:07:29,690
an app that scores over 600 companies daily out

164
00:07:29,690 --> 00:07:31,730
of 100 using the standards of great investors.

165
00:07:31,930 --> 00:07:35,343
Re-scored with the numbers of August 28, 2020,

166
00:07:35,343 --> 00:07:38,089
just before Berkshire's announcement,

167
00:07:38,089 --> 00:07:41,280
Itochu scored 90, with a price score of 99.

168
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:43,800
Today Itochu scores 48.

169
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,827
Its quality score is still 72,

170
00:07:46,827 --> 00:07:49,450
but its price score is 32.

171
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:53,010
The score blends four things: earning power as

172
00:07:53,010 --> 00:07:55,050
Buffett and Charlie Munger saw it,

173
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:57,450
a conservative Graham-style price check,

174
00:07:57,450 --> 00:08:00,150
qualitative factors like moat and management,

175
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:01,350
and industry trends.

176
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:05,685
Seventy or above is roughly the top 4%,

177
00:08:05,685 --> 00:08:08,330
so 90 back then was rare.

178
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:09,648
For reference,

179
00:08:09,648 --> 00:08:13,959
Itochu's yen share price has changed +352% since then.

180
00:08:13,959 --> 00:08:15,316
That's hindsight,

181
00:08:15,316 --> 00:08:18,030
not a sign the score predicted it.

182
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:20,518
Counting no future growth,

183
00:08:20,518 --> 00:08:23,262
using average earnings over several years,

184
00:08:23,262 --> 00:08:25,417
one Itochu share is worth 1,574엔.

185
00:08:25,417 --> 00:08:28,030
At the Oct 27 close it traded at 2,363엔.

186
00:08:28,230 --> 00:08:30,579
That's 1.5x the calculated value.

187
00:08:30,579 --> 00:08:34,281
The price tag now sits above the conservative value.

188
00:08:34,281 --> 00:08:35,776
Still a fine company,

189
00:08:35,776 --> 00:08:37,840
just not as cheap as in 2020.

190
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:40,620
Back to the first question.

191
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:42,450
Part three.

192
00:08:42,450 --> 00:08:47,880
A country's GDP rank is a report card measured in dollars.

193
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:50,854
A company's profit depends on where it earns,

194
00:08:50,854 --> 00:08:52,210
and in which currency.

195
00:08:52,410 --> 00:08:55,712
Judge a company only by headlines about its country's economy,

196
00:08:55,712 --> 00:08:57,470
and you've seen half the picture.

197
00:08:58,270 --> 00:09:05,488
One tip for your own money: in a company's annual report,

198
00:09:05,488 --> 00:09:13,340
find the revenue-by-region table and check the overseas share.

199
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:15,890
It tells you which currency the company really earns in.

200
00:09:16,090 --> 00:09:17,599
Big overseas sales mean a weak won lifts

201
00:09:17,599 --> 00:09:19,335
profits in won; mostly domestic sales mean the

202
00:09:19,335 --> 00:09:20,353
local economy matters more.

203
00:09:20,353 --> 00:09:21,523
Next time currency news breaks,

204
00:09:21,523 --> 00:09:22,730
ask which side your stock is on.

205
00:09:23,530 --> 00:09:26,076
Want more Warren Buffett stories? Subscribe,

206
00:09:26,076 --> 00:09:27,870
like and turn on notifications.

207
00:09:28,060 --> 00:09:30,148
Watch our Starbucks episode too,

208
00:09:30,148 --> 00:09:33,410
for one more way to read how companies make money.

209
00:09:33,610 --> 00:09:36,371
Does your stock earn in dollars or in won?

210
00:09:36,371 --> 00:09:39,330
Tell us in the comments. Thanks for watching.
